### Why do checked exceptions not work in lambdas?

The functional interfaces used by streams (`Function<T,R>`, `Consumer<T>`, `Predicate<T>`) have methods that do not declare any checked exception. If your lambda body calls a method that throws a checked exception, the compiler rejects it because the functional interface cannot propagate it.

### How do you handle it with inline try-catch?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.text.ParseException;
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.util.Date;
import java.util.List;

public class InlineTryCatch {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> dateStrings = List.of("2024-01-15", "2024-02-28", "invalid");

        List<Date> dates = dateStrings.stream()
            .map(s -> {
                try {
                    return new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd").parse(s);
                } catch (ParseException e) {
                    throw new RuntimeException("Failed to parse: " + s, e);
                }
            })
            .toList();
    }
}
```

This works but is verbose when repeated.

### How do you create a reusable wrapper?

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.function.Function;

@FunctionalInterface
public interface CheckedFunction<T, R> {
    R apply(T t) throws Exception;
}

public class StreamUtils {
    public static <T, R> Function<T, R> unchecked(CheckedFunction<T, R> f) {
        return t -> {
            try {
                return f.apply(t);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                throw new RuntimeException(e);
            }
        };
    }
}
```

Usage:

```java
import static com.bank.util.StreamUtils.unchecked;

List<Date> dates = dateStrings.stream()
    .map(unchecked(s -> new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd").parse(s)))
    .toList();
// Clean, readable, checked exception handled in one place
```

### How is inline try-catch different from the wrapper?

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Inline try-catch | No extra class | Verbose, repeated code |
| unchecked() wrapper | Clean call sites, reusable | One extra interface + utility class |
| Return Optional | No exception at all | Changes return type, needs downstream handling |

### What about expected failures?

When failure is expected (malformed records in a batch), prefer returning `Optional` or an `Either`-like result instead of throwing.

```java
List<Optional<Date>> results = dateStrings.stream()
    .map(s -> {
        try {
            return Optional.of(new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd").parse(s));
        } catch (ParseException e) {
            return Optional.<Date>empty();
        }
    })
    .toList();

// Filter to keep only successful parses
List<Date> successfulDates = results.stream()
    .flatMap(Optional::stream)
    .toList();
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: Losing the original exception cause.

```java
// WRONG: cause is lost
catch (Exception e) {
    throw new RuntimeException("parse failed");  // no cause!
}

// CORRECT: preserve the cause
catch (Exception e) {
    throw new RuntimeException("parse failed", e);  // cause preserved
}
```

### What about wrappers for Consumer and Supplier?

Make analogous wrappers for other functional interfaces.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface CheckedConsumer<T> { void accept(T t) throws Exception; }

static <T> java.util.function.Consumer<T> unchecked(CheckedConsumer<T> c) {
    return t -> {
        try { c.accept(t); }
        catch (Exception e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
    };
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Choosing between wrap-and-throw and result-objects depends on whether failure is exceptional or expected. For truly exceptional failures, the unchecked wrapper is clean. For expected per-element failures in batch processing, returning Optional or Either preserves partial results.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why can you not declare throws on a lambda?" The functional interface's method signature does not allow it.
- "When should you throw vs return Optional?" Throw for exceptional failures; return Optional for expected/recoverable ones.
- "How do you handle IOException specifically?" Use `UncheckedIOException` for the rethrow.
